  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: a4da58d2dc2409c459ef20449bf92a90, type: 3}
  m_Name: MaterialStress
  m_EditorClassIdentifier:
  lessonId: civil-craft-material-stress-lesson-v1
  lessonTitle: Material Stress
  lessonImage: {fileID: 0}
  popupDescription: "<b><#E09500>Material Stress</color></b> is the internal force per unit area developed within a material when it is subjected to loads.\n\nKey points:\n- Loads create internal stresses in structural members.\n- Stress can occur due to tension, compression, bending, or shear.\n- Different materials have different strengths and allowable stress limits.\n- Excessive stress can cause damage or structural failure.\n\nThis lesson has been added to your <b><#E09500>Almanac</color></b>."
  almanacImage: {fileID: 0}
  lessonDescription: "<b><#E09500>Material Stress</color></b> is the <b><#E09500>internal force per unit area</color></b> developed within a material under load. Loads placed on a bridge create internal stresses in its structural members. These stresses can occur due to <b><#E09500>tension, compression, bending, or shear</color></b>.\n\nDifferent materials can withstand different amounts of stress. Each material has its own strength and allowable stress limits. <b><#E09500>Concrete</color></b>, for example, generally handles compression well but is weaker in tension unless it is reinforced.\n\nIf the stress in a member exceeds what its material can safely withstand, the member may <b><#E09500>crack, deform excessively, or fail</color></b>. Choosing suitable materials and arranging the supports properly helps keep stresses within safe limits under the applied loads.\n\nFord's loaded cargo trucks carry heavier loads than the taxi from the previous project. During testing, watch how the bridge and its supports respond as a loaded truck moves across. Observe which members experience the greatest stress, and consider whether the supports or structural arrangement need adjustment."
